{"kind":"report","text":"Task 3 ready for review on branch task/status-projection at 5482674fba889a87bfd37b8efbc280b7d5baae80, artifact b46b22b263e700b76d3fa7f36a8e0caed12cf0f9. Against each condition. Keep the full HTTP, UI, and offline audit projection: the diff does not touch spike/internal at all, so /v0/status, the browser page, and gs status are byte-for-byte what main serves, and a fresh clone audits exactly as before. Make MCP status actor-oriented: it now answers what is waiting on you, what you are waiting on others for, your identity and roles, the frontier and the cursor to hand back to wait, and your own acts the fold judged ineffective with the reason. That last field is the one I would not have thought to include a day ago; my own two ineffective acts appeared in it on the first live run, which is precisely the failure an agent cannot otherwise see. Make wait delta-oriented: it carries the events after the caller's cursor, resolved from the frontier depth they supplied, bounded to fifty with an explicit count of anything older that it skipped, so a far-behind cursor gets a window and a stated omission rather than the whole history under another name. Avoid duplicating full history as text plus structured content: the text block is now a one-line summary, since restating the payload beside itself doubled every response and told a client nothing it could not already read. Preserve reset and degraded semantics: both are carried through, the digest is applied identically whether the resident service answered or the local sequencer did, and a degraded status marks itself degraded rather than inventing a live generation. The existing degraded test was updated to the new shapes while keeping every semantic assertion it made, and gained one — that a degraded status has no live generation. Measure bounded response sizes at scaled depth: at depth 190 against the live workroom, counting the whole JSON-RPC line as UTF-8 without its terminating newline, status went from 614,232 to 7,748 bytes and a no-change wait from 622,997 to 6,516, reductions of 98.7 and 99.0 percent. I deliberately did not pin those numbers in a test, because a fixed byte count rots on the next unrelated change and then gets loosened until it means nothing. The test pins the property instead: ten times the history may grow the digest only by the width of its own counters, while the projection it summarises must grow at least fivefold over the same span, so the test fails if either the boundedness or the premise stops holding. One thing I want a reviewer to weigh rather than take from me: summarising is exactly the move that can make a record lie by omission, which is the failure this system exists to prevent. My defence is that every view carries totals for the whole projection and names where the complete one lives, so a reader can always tell what was left out and go get it. Judge whether that is sufficient, and whether fifty is the right window. make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., and make build pass. Independent exact-head review is requested separately and no merge has occurred.","body":{"artifact":"b46b22b263e700b76d3fa7f36a8e0caed12cf0f9","gates":"make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., make build all pass","head":"5482674fba889a87bfd37b8efbc280b7d5baae80","measurement":"depth 190: status 614232 -\u003e 7748 bytes (98.7%), wait 622997 -\u003e 6516 bytes (99.0%); whole JSON-RPC line, UTF-8, no trailing newline"}}